utine testing  and i'm selecting this use case one  because i think it's of great interest  to this group  but also because it highlights the three  application areas  or the diversity of application areas  that pharmacogenetics demands  and here i'll define application in  genotyping copy number variation and  we'll talk about that in the context of  sip 2d6 analysis  and then hla analysis i bring this up  because in certain technologies  these can be addressed singularly in a  workflow  in other technologies they maybe  need to be addressed using different  application  technologies to support a full panel and  and yet each system that i'll define has  a  core value and so you'll want to assess  a platform selection and content  in the context of the type of panel you  want to  deploy the time frame that you need to  get results back to your constituents or  results back to your end user  cost is a key driver and certainly  your ability to customize that content  on an ongoing basis  i'll give an overview i think in kind of  a summary  of what i just described and i'll do  that in the context of the three  technology the two technologies that  i'll be describing and the three  uh panel-based use cases that i'll be  going through in the remainder of the  talk i'm going to start with a pcr  based technology in open array  and i'll define this as a core  genotyping platform  with copy number as a separate workflow  albeit on the same instrument  i see an application use case of less  than 120 markers  a time to results for just genotyping  would be as as  quickly as one day and genotyping with  copy number in two days  for the microarray based applications  i'll be talking about a relatively high  density  uh platform offering in pharmaco  scan which is about 4 000  variants covered over about a thousand  genes  a single workflow that can address copy  number genotyping  and hla in formats of 24 samples or 96  samples per plate  but a workflow of up to three and a half  days  and then a more targeted panel in  pharmacofocus also a microarray based  technology  covering again genotyping copper number  variation in hla in a single workflow  but as saying 2 000 markers  across about 150 genes but in in  a plate densities of 96 samples or 384  samples and again a workflow of about  four days  i'm going to start with the open array  workflow and for  some of you this may be very familiar uh  open array  represents what we would call a  mid-density  genotyping platform and it defines  really i think the largest  user group in a family of technologies  supporting pharmacogenetics today for  routine testing  as i mentioned in the slide prior  open array technology is primarily a  genotyping workflow  and you have a separate 384-well  workflow to support  copy number variation analysis to create  a full panel offering  so the pros here is that you have as low  as a four-hour  workflow from sample extraction to  result for just genotyping  the cons are that you would have to take  into consideration a separate workflow  for copy number variation  um in a 384 well uh pcr plate format  and if you wanted to add hla this would  require yet  a separate technology in sanger  sequencing  um to support an hla component for a  panel  so as you can see a platform selection  really has  a tremendous bearing on the type of  panel you can deploy  the time to result here a cost  per result which i'll talk about in a  moment  and uh and uh in an overall workflow  a little bit about the architecture of  the array for those who are unfamiliar  the open array can be formatted for  genotyping drawing from  a library of several million  pre-designed assays this is the tacman  genotyping assay portfolio  and in through a web-based formatting  application  that can be found on the thermo-fisher  scientific website  we can format this array drawing from  that content  in one of five formats so i can build an  array with 26  pharmacogenetic assays of interest  60 120 180 or 240  but as i described before open array is  is  is um much more relevant at 120 markers  and below  and if we look at these formats for  genotyping  we can see that at 26 markers we can  assay 96 samples  per run at 60 markers we can have say 48  at 120 markers  we can ask a 24 marker or 24 samples  per run the instrument the quant studio  12k  real time pcr instrument can thermal  cycle up to four  arrays every three hours so you can see  the sample throughput is pretty  significant  but we have to take that sample  throughput in the context of other panel  content  that would invoke other workflows like  copy number variation or hla  so our limiting factor would be defined  by  the lowest volume workflow  in that overall panel content  deliverable  for copy number variation analysis  workflow we draw from about one and a  half million  copy number variation assays that are  tiled across the entire human genome  now for applied testing we would  restrict consideration largely to sip  2d6 for pharmacogenetic application  and we have assay content that would  satisfy  a panel build for that type of  deployment  but again this would be a separate  workflow in a 384-well plate  i want to contrast the workflow that i  just described  in qpcr with microarray workflows  and i'm going to talk about two  different panel deployments  one is the axiom pharmacoscan array  versus the axiom pharmacofocus array  pharmacoscan of course being a much more  broad density array again four thousand  uh  four thousand pgx markers across  genotyping copy number variation in hla  versus the pharmacofocus array which is  about 2 000 markers of high  ptx value again across genotyping  copy number variation in hla content but  as you can see  sample formats are a big delineation  here  with pharmacoscan supporting plate  formats of 24 samples or 96 samples and  pharmacofocus  supporting plate formats of from 96 to  384 samples  again we're working across the same time  to result for both formats about three  and a half days  but our sample processivity on  pharmacofocus  is um is about 4x  increased over pharmaco scan  brief overview of the workflow um just  to  help you understand how the the daily  workflow is broken down  and day one we look at a multiplex pcr  that would be a combination of targeted  and full gene pcr  that's important for the full gene uh  pcr our amplification step  to interrogate highly complex genes like  zip 2d6 or 2c19  we would go into day two where we go  through fragmentation precipitation and  a full day of hybridization  in day three we label ligate wash and  scan and we're on to analysis  i'll talk a bit about the pharmaco scan  product in greater detail  as i mentioned we're looking at in  excess of  4 000 ptx  markers of high value so tremendous  breadth  copy number variation uh in the context  of the  lcd that i brought up earlier both  pharmacoscan and pharmacofocus would be  able to fully interrogate all of that  content  for pharmaco for pharmaco scan again  we're looking at  about a thousand genes uh covering cpic  high value content  uh pharm gkb content  of the highest evidence uh value all the  way down through  research grade value this next slide  breaks down that content  further and you can see the breakout of  admi content  uh historic team plus content  farm gkb content cpic guidelines and  so a full complement of content here  certainly enough to cover  the lcd we discussed earlier  in contrast the pharmacofocus array  narrows that content down to about 2 000  relevant ptx  variants across about 150 genes  again we're able to cover that high  value hla content that was described in  the lcd  as well the copy number variation uh  targets for  sub 2d6 that were just that were  described in the lcd  as well as copy number in hl8 content  well and above  what the lcd requires  so on the previous slide i highlighted  the overall content  description for pharmacofocus looking at  2000 high-value ptx variants across  applications in genotyping copy number  variation in hla  here we just show how pharmacofocus  addresses hla content certainly  the array addresses the lcd content that  was described earlier as our use case  and lastly we'll look at how  pharmacofocus  addresses copy number variation as you  can see  it addresses copy number variation for  genes well beyond  critical genes such as sip 2d6  which we described earlier as essential  for deployment for a note for the lcd  panel  that we saw earlier in the slides  so pharmaco focus interrogates  gene copy numbers for gstm1 utt2b17  salt1a1 to name a few  so taken together we've discussed two  technologies  qpcr-based technology that is fast but  is limited  uh compared to microarray and  application scope  and microarray technology that is broad  in content deployment  and certainly sufficient for the lcd  that we described from a panel  of support for routine testing but a bit  slower from the standpoint of return to  results  we've talked about content flexibility  overall workflow  again time to results it's important  that we highlight cost it's a key driver  so as a family open array  as a family of technologies that we  described as mid density  uh qpcr based platforms there are  several  open arrays one has  set the standard for being the really  the the most broadly used platform  technology in the marketplace for  routine testing  and as a family the community has become  used to  the testing community has become used to  a return of results between one to three  days depending on the breadth of  application  uh supported for panel they're used to  content  customizability and of which  all three use cases we described can  support  they're used to a price point for 120  markers and below of right around thirty  dollars per sample  pharmacoscan as the higher density  offering in the microwave family  offers at price point per result around  75 dollars per sample and below  what's important to note is that  pharmacofocus really bridges  these two family of products microarray  and qpcr  in that it offers a price point for  marker content that we describe of right  around  thirty dollars a sample and that means  as  a technology that had historically been  relegated to high density testing it's  now moved into this  higher volume mid-density family of  technologies  so hopefully i've been help i've been  able to help you understand some of the  selection criteria that we see the  the community using to pick technologies  we've talked a little bit about emerging  technologies and how  they're changing the dynamic of use of  technology in pharmacogenetics  and i thank you for your time

